    def default_owner(cls, v, info: ValidationInfo):
        return v or f"team-{info.data.get('env', 'dev')}"", "explanation": "The leftover v1 `@validator(..., always=True)` raised a NameError because `validator` is no longer imported. Per the Pydantic v2 migration guide, `always=True` maps to `validate_default=True`, and the default mode of v1 validators (after validation) maps to `mode="after"`. The method becomes a `@classmethod` under `@field_validator`, and the v1 `values` dict becomes `info: ValidationInfo` with `info.data`. `env` is declared before `owner`, so it is present in `info.data` as before. The `ValidationInfo` and `field_validator` imports already exist, so no import change is needed."}], "notes": "The previous attempt left the `percent` validator already migrated to `@field_validator` with `@classmethod`, and it has no v1 remnants, so it is unchanged. The `owner` validator was only partially migrated: it still used the v1 `@validator` decorator, which is undefined because `validator` is no longer imported. This edit converts it to the v2 form. The `from pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationInfo, field_validator` import already covers the new code. `Field` is currently unused, but it was not introduced by these edits and was not requested to be removed, so I left it alone. No documentation was retrieved, so the mapping (always -> validate_default, default mode -> after, values -> info.data) is based on the v2 migration rules given in the finding. The `rollout` `each_item=True` finding is addressed by the existing `@field_validator("rollout")` which validates the whole dict; that was an earlier correct migration and was preserved."}
